{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "`Def11.k3` asserts `φ → Kφ` instead of Reinhardt’s `Kφ → φ`. It omits the axiom that what is known is true and adds its converse, that whatever is true is known. The rule from `p` to `Bp` mentioned in P-R321 is not the object-language axiom `p → Bp`."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "`Def15.hyp` allows open formulas as hypotheses, whereas Reinhardt defines deducibility for a set `T` of sentences. This extends the domain of the definition without changing the hypothesis rule on its intended domain."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "`Def12` and `Def13` allow redundant or repeated quantifiers in a closing prefix. This is syntactically more permissive than the usual convention of forming a universal closure by binding just the free variables once each."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "As acknowledged in the claim, the relational fragment can instantiate only with variables and cannot express Leibniz’s law or the arithmetic schemas, including induction for formulas containing `K`."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The Lean captures the other three special schemas and correctly places the optional `K` outside the closed axiom. The reversal of `Kφ → φ`, however, makes the proof system materially different from Reinhardt’s."
}
